  "live_records": {
    "q-69979": {
      "dom_id": "step-q69979",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$G=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 6 & 7 & -2\\end{aligned}].$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the $4$th column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(G) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 6 & 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =(-2)\\cdot (1\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1))=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(G)=4.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
