    "q-130366": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130366",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $det(A)=5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\frac{1}{5}$\n\nII. $5\\text{det}({A}^{T})=1$\n\nIII. $\\text{det}({A}^{T})-3\\text{det}(I)=2$",
      "worked_solution": "From the properties of determinants, we know that\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)=\\text{det}({A}^{T})=5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=5\\ne \\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n5\\text{det}({A}^{T})=5\\cdot 5=25\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})-3\\text{det}(I)=5-3\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
