    "q-158370": {
      "dom_id": "step-q158370",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd column of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd column of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(15+21) \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|144|=144.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
